<section xml:id="sec-eigenvectors">
  <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>

  <objectives>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Learn the definition of eigenvector and eigenvalue.</p></li>
      <li><p>Learn to find eigenvectors and eigenvalues geometrically.</p></li>
      <li><p>Learn to decide if a number is an eigenvalue of a matrix, and if so, how to find an associated eigenvector.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Recipe:</em> find a basis for the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Pictures:</em> whether or not a vector is an eigenvector, eigenvectors of standard matrix transformations.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Theorem:</em> the expanded invertible matrix theorem.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Vocabulary word:</em> <term>eigenspace</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Essential vocabulary words:</em> <term>eigenvector</term>, <term>eigenvalue</term>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section, we define eigenvalues and eigenvectors.  These form the most important facet of the structure theory of square matrices.  As such, eigenvalues and eigenvectors tend to play a key role in the real-life applications of linear algebra.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-eigenvalues-and-eigenvectors">
    <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>

    <p>
      Here is the most important definition in this text.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>eigenvector for</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvector</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvector</h><h>eigenvalue for</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <sidebyside widths="5% 90%"><image source="images/important.svg" width="8%">
          <shortdescription>Essential definition</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix.
          <ol>
            <li><p>
              An <term>eigenvector</term> of <m>A</m> is a <em>nonzero</em> vector <m>v</m> in <m>\R^n</m> such that <m>Av=\lambda v</m>, for some scalar <m>\lambda</m>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              An <term>eigenvalue</term> of <m>A</m> is a scalar <m>\lambda</m> such that the equation <m>Av=\lambda v</m> has a <em>nontrivial</em> solution.
            </p></li>
          </ol>
          If <m>Av = \lambda v</m> for <m>v\neq 0</m>, we say that <m>\lambda</m> is the <term>eigenvalue for</term> <m>v</m>, and that <m>v</m> is an <term>eigenvector for</term> <m>\lambda</m>.
        </p></sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The German prefix <q>eigen</q> roughly translates to <q>self</q> or <q>own</q>.  An eigenvector of <m>A</m> is a vector that is taken to a multiple of itself by the matrix transformation <m>T(x)=Ax</m>, which perhaps explains the terminology.  On the other hand, <q>eigen</q> is often translated as <q>characteristic</q>; we may think of an eigenvector as describing an intrinsic, or characteristic, property of <m>A</m>.  
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        Eigenvalues and eigenvectors are only for square matrices.
      </p>
    </note>

    <insight>
      <p>
        Eigenvectors are <em>by definition nonzero</em>.  Eigenvalues may be equal to zero.
      </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
      We do not consider the zero vector to be an eigenvector: since <m>A0 = 0 = \lambda 0</m> for <em>every</em> scalar <m>\lambda</m>, the associated eigenvalue would be undefined.
    </p>

	<p>
If someone hands you a matrix <m>A</m> and a vector <m>v</m>, it is easy to check if <m>v</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>: simply multiply <m>v</m> by <m>A</m> and see if <m>Av</m> is a scalar multiple of <m>v</m>. On the other hand, given just the matrix <m>A</m>, it is not obvious at all how to find the eigenvectors.  We will learn how to do this in <xref ref="sec-characteristic-polynomial" text="type-global"/>.
	</p>

    <example>
      <title>Verifying eigenvectors</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 2 \\
-4 \amp 8
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{and vectors} v = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}  \qquad w = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Which are eigenvectors?  What are their eigenvalues?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
      <p>
        We have
        <md>
<mrow>Av = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 2 \\
-4 \amp 8
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
4
\end{pmatrix} = 4v</mrow>
</md>.
        Hence, <m>v</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>, with eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 4</m>.  On the other hand,
        <md>
<mrow>Aw = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 2 \\
-4 \amp 8
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
6 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        which is not a scalar multiple of <m>w</m>.  Hence, <m>w</m> is not an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, baseline=1.2cm, scale=.5]
  \draw[grid lines] (-1, -1) grid (7, 6);
  \draw[seq-red] (-1, -1) -- (6, 6);
  \draw[seq-blue] (-1/2, -1) -- (3, 6);
  \point[seq-red, "$v$" below right] at (1, 1);
  \point[seq-red, "$Av$" below right] at (4, 4);
  \point[seq-blue, "$w$" above left] at (1, 2);
  \point[seq-blue, "$Aw$"] at (6, 0);
  \point["0" above left] at  (0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}\qquad
\begin{minipage}[c]{.3\linewidth}
  $\color{seq-red}v$ is an eigenvector\\[5mm]
  $\color{seq-blue}w$ is not an eigenvector
\end{minipage}
        </latex-image>
</image>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Verifying eigenvectors</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 6 \amp 8 \\
\frac \amp 12 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \frac \amp 12 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}
  \sptxt{and vectors} v = \begin{pmatrix}
16 \\
4 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad w = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
2 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Which are eigenvectors?  What are their eigenvalues?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
      <p>
        We have
        <md>
<mrow>Av = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 6 \amp 8 \\
\frac \amp 12 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \frac \amp 12 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
16 \\
4 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
    = \begin{pmatrix}
32 \\
8 \\
2
\end{pmatrix} = 2v</mrow>
</md>.
        Hence, <m>v</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>, with eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 2</m>.  On the other hand,
        <md>
<mrow>Aw = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 6 \amp 8 \\
\frac \amp 12 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \frac \amp 12 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
2 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}
          = \begin{pmatrix}
28 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
        which is not a scalar multiple of <m>w</m>.  Hence, <m>w</m> is not an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.
      </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>An eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>0</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 3 \\
2 \amp 6
\end{pmatrix} \qquad v = \begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Is <m>v</m> an eigenvector of <m>A</m>?  If so, what is its eigenvalue?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The product is
          <md>
<mrow>Av = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 3 \\
2 \amp 6
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} = 0v</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence, <m>v</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <em>zero</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          As noted above, an eigen<em>value</em> is allowed to be zero, but an eigen<em>vector</em> is not.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      <idx><h>Eigenvector</h><h>and collinearity</h></idx>
    To say that <m>Av=\lambda v</m> means that <m>Av</m> and <m>\lambda v</m> are <em>collinear with the origin</em>.  So, an eigenvector of <m>A</m> is a nonzero vector <m>v</m> such that <m>Av</m> and <m>v</m> lie on the same line through the origin.  In this case, <m>Av</m> is a scalar multiple of <m>v</m>; the eigenvalue is the scaling factor.
    </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) rectangle (2,2);
  \clip (-2,-2) rectangle (2,2);
  \coordinate (v) at (1/2,1/3);
  \draw[seq-red!70] ($-5*(v)$) -- ($5*(v)$);
  \point[seq-red, "$v$"] at (v);
  \point[seq-red, "$Av$"] at ($2.5*(v)$);
  \coordinate (w) at (-1/3,1);
  \draw[seq-blue!70] ($-5*(w)$) -- ($5*(w)$);
  \point[seq-blue, "$w$" above right] (w) at (w);
  \point[seq-blue, "$Aw$"] at (-1.2, 1);
  \point["0" {anchor=350, inner sep=3pt}] at  (0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}\qquad
\begin{minipage}[c]{.3\linewidth}
  $\color{seq-red}v$ is an eigenvector\\[5mm]
  $\color{seq-blue}w$ is not an eigenvector
\end{minipage}
    </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
    For matrices that arise as the standard matrix of a linear transformation, it is often best to draw a picture, then find the eigenvectors and eigenvalues geometrically by studying which vectors are not moved off of their line.  For a transformation that is defined geometrically, it is not necessary even to compute its matrix to find the eigenvectors and eigenvalues.
    </p>

    <observation xml:id="evec-eg-reflection">
      <title>Reflection</title>
      <idx><h>Reflection</h><h>eigenvectors of</h></idx>
        <p>
          Here is an example of this.  Let <m>T\colon\R^2\to\R^2</m> be the linear transformation that reflects over the line <m>L</m> defined by <m>y=-x</m>, and let <m>A</m> be the matrix for <m>T</m>.  We will find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m> without doing any computations.
        </p>
        <p>
          This transformation is defined geometrically, so we draw a picture.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \draw[very thin, seq-violet, dashed] (-2,2) to["$L$" near end] (2,-2);
  \draw[<->, seq-violet!70] (1.5,-1.05) to[bend left] (1.05,-1.5);

  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \draw[seq1!50, -, shorten=0pt, thick] (0,2) -- (0,-2);
  \point[seq1, "$u$" {left,text=seq1}] (a) at (0,-1);
  \point[seq1, "$Au$" {above right, text=seq1}] (Ta) at (1,0);
  \draw (a) -- (Ta);
  \point["0" below left] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
  ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\mh{u}</m> is not an eigenvector, because <m>Au</m> is not collinear with <m>u</m> and the origin.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \draw[very thin, seq-violet, dashed] (-2,2) to["$L$" near end] (2,-2);
  \draw[<->, seq-violet!70] (1.5,-1.05) to[bend left] (1.05,-1.5);

  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \draw[seq6!50, -, shorten=0pt, thick] (2/3,2) -- (-2/3,-2);
  \point[seq6, "$z$" {right, text=seq6}] (a) at (.5,1.5);
  \point[seq6, "$Az$" {below right, text=seq6}] (Ta) at (-1.5,-.5);
  \draw (a) -- (Ta);
  \point["0" left] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
  ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\color{seq6}z</m> is not an eigenvector either.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \draw[very thin, seq-violet, dashed] (-2,2) to["$L$" near end] (2,-2);
  \draw[<->, seq-violet!70] (1.5,-1.05) to[bend left] (1.05,-1.5);

  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \draw[seq2!50, -, shorten=0pt, thick] (2,2) -- (-2,-2);
  \point[seq2, "$v$" {above left, text=seq2}] (b) at (1,1);
  \point[seq2, "$Av$" {text=seq2, below right}] (Tb) at (-1,-1);
  \draw (b) -- (Tb);
  \point["0" left] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
  ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\color{seq2}v</m> is an eigenvector because <m>Av</m> is collinear with <m>v</m> and the origin.  The vector <m>Av</m> has the same length as <m>v</m>, but the opposite direction, so the associated eigenvalue is <m>-1</m>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \draw[very thin, seq-violet, dashed] (-2,2) to["$L$" near end] (2,-2);
  \draw[<->, seq-violet!70] (1.5,-1.05) to[bend left] (1.05,-1.5);

  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \draw[seq3!50, -, shorten=0pt, thick] (-2,2) -- (2,-2);
  \point[seq3, "$w$" {text=seq3,inner sep=3pt, anchor=270}] (c) at (-1,1);
  \point[seq3, "$Aw$" {text=seq3,inner sep=3pt, anchor=180}] (c) at (-1,1);
  \draw (c) to[out=190, in=260, looseness=40] (c);
  \point["0" below left] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
  ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\color{seq3}w</m> is an eigenvector because <m>Aw</m> is collinear with <m>w</m> and the origin: indeed, <m>Aw</m> is equal to <m>w</m>!  This means that <m>w</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          It appears that all eigenvectors lie either on <m>L</m>, or on the line perpendicular to <m>L</m>.  The vectors on <m>L</m> have eigenvalue <m>1</m>, and the vectors perpendicular to <m>L</m> have eigenvalue <m>-1</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>An eigenvector of <m>A</m> is a vector <m>x</m> such that <m>Ax</m> is collinear with <m>x</m> and the origin.  Click and drag the head of <m>x</m> to convince yourself that all such vectors lie either on <m>L</m>, or on the line perpendicular to <m>L</m>.</caption>
