    </observation>

	<p>
	We will now give five more examples of this nature
	</p>

    <example xml:id="evec-eg-projection">
      <title>Projection</title>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>eigenvectors of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T\colon\R^2\to\R^2</m> be the linear transformation that projects a vector vertically onto the <m>x</m>-axis, and let <m>A</m> be the matrix for <m>T</m>.  Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m> without doing any computations.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This transformation is defined geometrically, so we draw a picture.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \draw[seq-violet, dashed, thick] (-2,0) to (2,0);

  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \draw[seq1!50, -, shorten=0pt, thick] (2,-2) -- (-2,2);
  \point[seq1, "$u$" {left,text=seq1}] (a) at (1,-1);
  \point[seq1, "$Au$" {above right, text=seq1}] (Ta) at (1,0);
  \draw (a) -- (Ta);
  \point["0" below left] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
  ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\mh{u}</m> is not an eigenvector, because <m>Au</m> is not collinear with <m>u</m> and the origin.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
