  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \draw[seq6!50, -, shorten=0pt, thick] (2/3,2) -- (-2/3,-2);
  \point[seq6, "$z$" {right, text=seq6}] (a) at (-.5,-1.5);
  \point[seq6, "$Az$" {above, text=seq6}] (Ta) at (-.5,0);
  \draw (a) -- (Ta);
  \point["0" below right] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
  ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\color{seq6}z</m> is not an eigenvector either.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \draw[seq-violet, dashed, thick] (-2,0) to (2,0);

  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \draw[seq2!50, -, shorten=0pt, thick] (0,2) -- (0,-2);
  \point[seq2, "$v$" {above left, text=seq2}] (b) at (0,1);
  \point[seq2, "$Av$" {text=seq2, below right}] (Tb) at (0,0);
  \draw (b) -- (Tb);
  \point["0" below left] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
  ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\color{seq2}v</m> is an eigenvector.  Indeed, <m>Av</m> is the zero vector, which is collinear with <m>v</m> and the origin; since <m>Av = 0v</m>, the associated eigenvalue is <m>0</m>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \draw[seq-violet, dashed, thick] (-2,0) to (2,0);

  \begin{scope}[arrows={|->}, shorten=2pt, font=\small]
  \point[seq3, "$w$" {text=seq3,inner sep=3pt, anchor=-35}] (c) at (1,0);
  \point[seq3, "$Aw$" {text=seq3,inner sep=3pt, anchor=-145}] (c) at (1,0);
  \draw (c) to[out=-125, in=-55, looseness=40] (c);
  \point["0" below left] at (0, 0);
  \end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          The vector <m>\color{seq3}w</m> is an eigenvector because <m>Aw</m> is collinear with <m>w</m> and the origin: indeed, <m>Aw</m> is equal to <m>w</m>!  This means that <m>w</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          It appears that all eigenvectors lie on the <m>x</m>-axis or the <m>y</m>-axis.  The vectors on the <m>x</m>-axis have eigenvalue <m>1</m>, and the vectors on the <m>y</m>-axis have eigenvalue <m>0</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>An eigenvector of <m>A</m> is a vector <m>x</m> such that <m>Ax</m> is collinear with <m>x</m> and the origin.  Click and drag the head of <m>x</m> to convince yourself that all such vectors lie on the coordinate axes.</caption>
