    <example xml:id="evecs-eg-shear">
      <title>Shear</title>
      <idx><h>Shear</h><h>eigenvectors of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          and let <m>T(x) = Ax</m>, so <m>T</m> is a shear in the <m>x</m>-direction.  Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m> without doing any computations.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          In equations, we have
          <md>
<mrow>A\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
x+y \\
y
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          This tells us that a shear takes a vector and adds its <m>y</m>-coordinate to its <m>x</m>-coordinate.  Since the <m>x</m>-coordinate changes but not the <m>y</m>-coordinate, this tells us that any vector <m>v</m> with nonzero <m>y</m>-coordinate cannot be collinear with <m>Av</m> and the origin.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, scale=1, baseline]
  \draw[grid lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);

  \draw[seq2] (-1,-2) -- (1,2);
  \point[seq2, "$v$" {above left, text=seq2}] (b) at (.5,1);
  \point[seq2, "$Av$" {text=seq2, below}] (Tb) at (1.5,1);
  \draw[|->, shorten=2pt] (b) -- (Tb);
  \point["0" above left] at (0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          On the other hand, any vector <m>v</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis has zero <m>y</m>-coordinate, so it is not moved by <m>A</m>.  Hence <m>v</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>1</m>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
