      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      Here we mention one basic fact about eigenvectors.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="evecs-linindep">
      <title>Eigenvectors with distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent</title>
      <idx><h>Eigenvector</h><h>linear independence of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k</m> be eigenvectors of a matrix <m>A</m>, and suppose that the corresponding eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\ldots,\lambda_k</m> are <em>distinct</em> (all different from each other).  Then <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m> is linearly independent.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Suppose that <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m> were linearly <em>de</em>pendent.  According to the <xref ref="linindep-increasing-span">increasing span criterion</xref>, this means that for some <m>j</m>, the vector <m>v_j</m> is in <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_{j-1}\}.</m>  If we choose the <em>first</em> such <m>j</m>, then <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_{j-1}\}</m> is linearly independent.  Note that <m>j > 1</m> since <m>v_1\neq 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>v_j</m> is in <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_{j-1}\},</m>, we can write
          <md>
<mrow>v_j = c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \cdots + c_{j-1}v_{j-1}</mrow>
</md>
          for some scalars <m>c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_{j-1}</m>.  Multiplying both sides of the above equation by <m>A</m> gives
          <md>
<mrow>\lambda_jv_j =
            Av_j \amp= A\bigl(c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \cdots + c_{j-1}v_{j-1}\bigr)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= c_1Av_1 + c_2Av_2 + \cdots + c_{j-1}Av_{j-1}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= c_1\lambda_1v_1 + c_2\lambda_2v_2 + \cdots + c_{j-1}\lambda_{j-1}v_{j-1}</mrow>
</md>.
          Subtracting <m>\lambda_j</m> times the first equation from the second gives
          <md>
<mrow>0 = \lambda_jv_j - \lambda_jv_j
            = c_1(\lambda_1-\lambda_j)v_1 + c_2(\lambda_2-\lambda_j)v_2 + \cdots + c_{j-1}(\lambda_{j-1}-\lambda_j)v_{j-1}</mrow>
</md>.
          Since <m>\lambda_i\neq\lambda_j</m> for <m>i \lt j</m>, this is an equation of linear dependence among <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_{j-1}</m>, which is impossible because those vectors are linearly independent.  Therefore, <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m> must have been linearly independent after all.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <p>
      When <m>k=2</m>, this says that if <m>v_1,v_2</m> are eigenvectors with eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1\neq\lambda_2</m>, then <m>v_2</m> is not a multiple of <m>v_1</m>.  In fact, any nonzero multiple <m>cv_1</m> of <m>v_1</m> is also an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1</m>:
      <md>
<mrow>A(cv_1) = cAv_1 = c(\lambda_1 v_1) = \lambda_1(cv_1)</mrow>
</md>.
    </p>

    <p>
      As a consequence of the above <xref ref="evecs-linindep"/>, we have the following.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="evecs-max-nr">
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>maximum number of</h></idx>
      <p>
        An <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> has at most <m>n</m> eigenvalues.
      </p>
    </insight>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-eigenspaces">
    <title>Eigenspaces</title>

	<p>
	Suppose that <m>A</m> is a square matrix.  We already know how to check if a given vector is an eigenvector of <m>A</m> and in that case to find the eigenvalue.  Our next goal is to check if a given real number is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> and in that case to find all of the corresponding eigenvectors.  Again this will be straightforward, but more involved.  The only missing piece, then, will be to find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>; this is the main content of <xref ref="sec-characteristic-polynomial" text="type-global"/>.
	</p>

    <p>
      Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and let <m>\lambda</m> be a scalar.  The eigenvectors with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>, if any, are the nonzero solutions of the equation <m>Av=\lambda v</m>.  We can rewrite this equation as follows:
      <md>
<mrow>\amp Av = \lambda v</mrow>
<mrow>\iff\quad \amp Av - \lambda v = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\iff\quad \amp Av - \lambda I_nv = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\iff\quad \amp(A - \lambda I_n)v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
      Therefore, the eigenvectors of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>, if any, are the nontrivial solutions of the matrix equation <m>(A-\lambda I_n)v = 0</m>, i.e., the nonzero vectors in <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_n)</m>.  If this equation has no nontrivial solutions, then <m>\lambda</m> is not an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <idx><h>Eigenspace</h><h>is a solution set</h></idx>
      The above observation is important because it says that <em>finding the eigenvectors for a given eigenvalue means solving a homogeneous system of equations.</em>  For instance, if
      <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
7 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
-3 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
-3 \amp -2 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
      then an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> is a nontrivial solution of the matrix equation
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
7 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
-3 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
-3 \amp -2 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix} = \lambda \begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
      This translates to the system of equations
      <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{alignedat}{4}
7x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 3z \amp {}={} \amp \lambda x \amp {} \\
-3x \amp {}+{} \amp 2y \amp {}-{} \amp 3z \amp {}={} \amp \lambda y \amp {} \\
-3x \amp {}-{} \amp 2y \amp {}-{} \amp z \amp {}={} \amp \lambda z \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
        \quad\xrightarrow{\phantom{MMM}}\quad
        \left\{\begin{alignedat}{6}
(7 \amp {}-{} \amp \lambda)x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 3z \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp {} \\
-3x \amp {}+{} \amp (2 \amp {}-{} \amp \lambda)y \amp {}-{} \amp 3z \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp {} \\
-3x \amp {}-{} \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp ( \amp {}-{} \amp 1 \amp {}-{} \amp \lambda)z \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right</mrow>
</md>.
      This is the same as the homogeneous matrix equation
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
7-\lambda \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
-3 \amp 2-\lambda \amp -3 \\
-3 \amp -2 \amp -1-\lambda
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix} = 0</mrow>
</md>,
      i.e., <m>(A-\lambda I_3)v = 0</m>.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Eigenspace</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and let <m>\lambda</m> be an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.  The <term><m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace</term> of <m>A</m> is the solution set of <m>(A-\lambda I_n)v=0</m>, i.e., the subspace <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_n)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      <idx><h>Eigenspace</h><h>is a subspace</h></idx>
      The <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace is a subspace because it is the null space of a matrix, namely, the matrix <m>A-\lambda I_n</m>.  This subspace consists of the zero vector and all eigenvectors of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>.
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        Since a nonzero subspace is infinite, <em>every eigenvalue has infinitely many eigenvectors</em>.  (For example, multiplying an eigenvector by a nonzero scalar gives another eigenvector.)  On the other hand, there can be at most <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors of an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, since <m>\R^n</m> has dimension <m>n</m>.
      </p>
    </note>

    <example>
      <title>Computing eigenspaces</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the numbers <m>\lambda = -2, 1, 3</m>, decide if <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          and if so, compute a basis for the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The number <m>3</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if <m>\Nul(A-3I_2)</m> is nonzero.  Hence, we have to solve the matrix equation <m>(A-3I_2)v = 0</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>A - 3I_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} - 3\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp -4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The reduced row echelon form of this matrix is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 4 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x \amp {}={} \amp -4y \amp {} \\
y \amp {}={} \amp y \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{vector form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix} = y\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Since <m>y</m> is a free variable, the null space of <m>A-3I_2</m> is nonzero, so <m>3</m> is an eigenvector.  A basis for the <m>3</m>-eigenspace is <m>\bigl\{{-4\choose 1}\bigr\}.</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Concretely, we have shown that the eigenvectors of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>3</m> are exactly the nonzero multiples of <m>{-4\choose 1}</m>.  In particular, <m>-4\choose 1</m> is an eigenvector, which we can verify:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
-12 \\
3
\end{pmatrix} = 3\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number <m>1</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if <m>\Nul(A-I_2)</m> is nonzero.  Hence, we have to solve the matrix equation <m>(A-I_2)v = 0</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>A - I_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          This matrix has determinant <m>-6</m>, so it is invertible.  By the <xref ref="imt-1"/>, we have <m>\Nul(A-I_2) = \{0\}</m>, so <m>1</m> is not an eigenvalue.
        </p>
        <p>
          The eigenvectors of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>-2</m>, if any, are the nonzero solutions of the matrix equation <m>(A+2I_2)v = 0</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>A + 2I_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} + 2\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
4 \amp -4 \\
-1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The reduced row echelon form of this matrix is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x \amp {}={} \amp y \amp {} \\
y \amp {}={} \amp y \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{vector form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix} = y\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence there exist eigenvectors with eigenvalue <m>-2</m>, namely, any nonzero multiple of <m>{1\choose 1}.</m>  A basis for the <m>-2</m>-eigenspace is <m>\bigl\{{1\choose 1}\bigr\}.</m>
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The <m>3</m>-eigenspace is the line spanned by <m>{-4\choose 1}.</m>  This means that <m>A</m> scales every vector in that line by a factor of <m>3</m>.  Likewise, the <m>-2</m>-eigenspace is the line spanned by <m>{1\choose 1}.</m>  Click and drag the vector <m>x</m> around to see how <m>A</m> acts on that vector.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=2,-4:-1,-1&amp;nomult"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Computing eigenspaces</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the numbers <m>\lambda=0, \frac 12, 2</m>, decide if <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvector of the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
7/2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
-3/2 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
-3/2 \amp 0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          and if so, compute a basis for the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The number <m>2</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if <m>\Nul(A-2I_3)</m> is nonzero.  Hence, we have to solve the matrix equation <m>(A-2I_3)v = 0</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>A - 2I_3 = \begin{pmatrix}
7/2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
-3/2 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
-3/2 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} - 2\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            = \begin{pmatrix}
3/2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
-3/2 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
-3/2 \amp 0 \amp -3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The reduced row echelon form of this matrix is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x \amp {}={} \amp -2z \amp {} \\
y \amp {}={} \amp y \amp {} \\
z \amp {}={} \amp z \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{vector form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix} = y\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + z\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The matrix <m>A-2I_3</m> has two free variables, so the null space of <m>A-2I_3</m> is nonzero, and thus <m>2</m> is an eigenvector.  A basis for the <m>2</m>-eigenspace is
          <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\,\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          This is a <em>plane</em> in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The eigenvectors of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>\frac 12</m>, if any, are the nonzero solutions of the matrix equation <m>(A-\frac 12I_3)v = 0</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>A - \frac 12I_3 = \begin{pmatrix}
7/2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
-3/2 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
-3/2 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} - \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            = \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
-3/2 \amp 3/2 \amp -3 \\
-3/2 \amp 0 \amp -3/2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The reduced row echelon form of this matrix is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x \amp {}={} \amp -z \amp {} \\
y \amp {}={} \amp z \amp {} \\
z \amp {}={} \amp z \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{vector form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix} = z\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence there exist eigenvectors with eigenvalue <m>\frac 12</m>, so <m>\frac 12</m> is an eigenvalue.  A basis for the <m>\frac 12</m>-eigenspace is
          <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          This is a <em>line</em> in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number <m>0</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if <m>\Nul(A-0I_3) = \Nul(A)</m> is nonzero.  This is the same as asking whether <m>A</m> is noninvertible, by the <xref ref="imt-1"/>.  The determinant of <m>A</m> is <m>\det(A) = 2\neq 0</m>, so <m>A</m> is invertible by the <xref ref="det-defn-invert-prop">invertibility property</xref>.  It follows that <m>0</m> is not an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The <m>2</m>-eigenspace is the violet plane.  This means that <m>A</m> scales every vector in that plane by a factor of <m>2</m>.  The <m>\frac 12</m>-eigenspace is the green line.  Click and drag the vector <m>x</m> around to see how <m>A</m> acts on that vector.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=7/2,0,3:-3/2,2,-3:-3/2,0,-1&amp;nomult"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Reflection</title>
      <idx><h>Reflection</h><h>eigenspaces of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T\colon\R^2\to\R^2</m> be the linear transformation that reflects over the line <m>L</m> defined by <m>y=-x</m>, and let <m>A</m> be the matrix for <m>T</m>.  Find all eigenspaces of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We showed in <xref ref="evec-eg-reflection"/> that all eigenvectors with eigenvalue <m>1</m> lie on <m>L</m>, and all eigenvectors with eigenvalue <m>-1</m> lie on the line <m>L^\perp</m> that is perpendicular to <m>L</m>.  Hence, <m>L</m> is the <m>1</m>-eigenspace, and <m>L^\perp</m> is the <m>-1</m>-eigenspace.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of this required any computations, but we can verify our conclusions using algebra.  First we compute the matrix <m>A</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>T\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            T\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} \quad\implies\quad
            A = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Computing the <m>1</m>-eigenspace means solving the matrix equation <m>(A-I_2)v=0</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>A-I_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}
            \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of the solution set is <m>x = -y</m>, or equivalently, <m>y = -x</m>, which is exactly the equation for <m>L</m>. Computing the <m>-1</m>-eigenspace means solving the matrix equation <m>(A+I_2)v=0</m>; we have
          <md>
<mrow>A+I_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of the solution set is <m>x = y</m>, or equivalently, <m>y = x</m>, which is exactly the equation for <m>L^\perp</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The violet line <m>L</m> is the <m>1</m>-eigenspace, and the green line <m>L^\perp</m> is the <m>-1</m>-eigenspace.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=0,-1:-1,0&amp;nomult"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <insight>
      <title>Recipes: Eigenspaces</title>
      <idx><h>Eigenspace</h><h>computation</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvector</h><h>computation</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix and let <m>\lambda</m> be a number.
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if <m>(A-\lambda I_n)v = 0</m> has a nontrivial solution, if and only if <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_n)\neq\{0\}.</m>
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            In this case, finding a basis for the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> means finding a basis for <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_n)</m>, which can be done by finding the parametric vector form of the solutions of the homogeneous system of equations <m>(A-\lambda I_n)v = 0</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            The dimension of the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is equal to the number of free variables in the system of equations <m>(A-\lambda I_n)v = 0</m>, which is the number of columns of <m>A - \lambda I_n</m> without pivots.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            The eigenvectors with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> are the nonzero vectors in <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_n),</m> or equivalently, the nontrivial solutions of <m>(A-\lambda I_n)v = 0</m>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
      We conclude with an observation about the <m>0</m>-eigenspace of a matrix.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="evecs-eval0">
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>zero</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenspace</h><h>and the null space</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Null space</h><h>is the <m>0</m>-eigenspace</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix.
          <ol>
            <li><p>
              The number <m>0</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if <m>A</m> is not invertible.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              In this case, the <m>0</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is <m>\Nul(A)</m>.
            </p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof visible="true">
        <p>
          We know that <m>0</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if <m>\Nul(A - 0I_n) = \Nul(A)</m> is nonzero, which is equivalent to the noninvertibility of <m>A</m> by the <xref ref="imt-1"/>.  In this case, the <m>0</m>-eigenspace is by definition <m>\Nul(A-0I_n) = \Nul(A)</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <p>
      Concretely, an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>0</m> is a nonzero vector <m>v</m> such that <m>Av=0v</m>, i.e., such that <m>Av = 0</m>.  These are exactly the nonzero vectors in the null space of <m>A</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-invertible-matrix-theorem-addenda">
    <title>The Invertible Matrix Theorem: Addenda</title>

    <p>
      We now have two new ways of saying that a matrix is invertible, so we add them to the <xref ref="imt-1" text="title">invertible matrix theorem</xref>.
    </p>

   <theorem xml:id="imt-2">
     <idx><h>Invertible matrix theorem</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Invertible matrix</h><h>invertible matrix theorem</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Invertible transformation</h><h>and invertible matrices</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Reduced row echelon form</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>System of linear equations</h><h>trivial solution</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>System of linear equations</h><h>nontrivial solution</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Null space</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Linear independence</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>One-to-one</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Onto</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Matrix equation</h><h>always consistent</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Column space</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Invertible matrix</h><h>determinant of</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Determinant</h><h>invertibility property</h></idx>
     <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>and invertibility</h></idx>
     <statement>
       <p>
         Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and let <m>T\colon\R^n\to\R^n</m> be the matrix transformation <m>T(x) = Ax</m>.  The following statements are equivalent:
         <ol>
           <li><p><m>A</m> is invertible.</p></li>
           <li><p><m>A</m> has <m>n</m> pivots.</p></li>
           <li><p><m>\Nul(A) = \{0\}</m>.</p></li>
           <li><p>The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.</p></li>

           <li><p>The columns of <m>A</m> span <m>\R^n</m>.</p></li>
           <li><p><m>Ax=b</m> has a unique solution for each <m>b</m> in <m>\R^n</m>.</p></li>

           <li><p><m>T</m> is invertible.</p></li>
           <li><p><m>T</m> is one-to-one.</p></li>
           <li><p><m>T</m> is onto.</p></li>
           <li><p><m>\det(A) \neq 0</m>.</p></li>
           <li><p><m>0</m> is not an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
         </ol>
       </p>
     </statement>
   </theorem>

  </subsection>
<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>\det(A)=2</m>, then <m>2</m> is an
      eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  For example, <m>A=\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2\end{pmatrix}</m> has <m>\det(A)=2</m> but its eigenvalues
        are <m>4</m> and <m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are nonzero
      vectors in <m>{\bf R}^{n}</m> satisfying <m>Au = 2u</m> and <m>Av = 3v</m>.  Then <m>\{u, v\}</m> must
      be linearly independent.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: we have implicitly been told that <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> are eigenvalues of <m>A</m>,
        where <m>u</m> is a <m>2</m>-eigenvector and <m>v</m> is a <m>3</m>-eigenvector.  Since eigenvectors
        corresponding to different eigenvalues are automatically linearly independent,
        the set <m>\{u,v\}</m> must be linearly independent.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>The matrices <m>A=\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>B=\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 20 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m> have
      the same eigenvalues.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  Each matrix has eigenvalues <m>\lambda=2</m> and <m>\lambda = -1</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>v</m> and <m>w</m> are eigenvectors
      of <m>A</m>, then <m>v+w</m> is also an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  For example, if <m>A= \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</m> then <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>
        are eigenvectors, but <m>A \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> so <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> is not an
        eigenvector.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>The zero vector is an eigenvector for every square matrix.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  By definition of eigenvector, the zero vector is never an eigenvector
        for any matrix.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Which of the following vectors (if any) are eigenvectors for
      <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix}</m>?</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 5\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We check each vector one-by-one.
        <md><mrow>Av_1 = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}38 \\ 43\end{pmatrix},
          \quad \textup{which is not a scalar multiple of } v_1</mrow></md>.
        <md><mrow>Av_2 = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ -3\end{pmatrix} = 3v_2</mrow></md>.
        <md><mrow>Av_3 = \begin{pmatrix}35 \amp -32 \\ 40 \amp -37\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 5\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-20 \\ -25\end{pmatrix} = -5v_3</mrow></md>.
        From the
        computations, we conclude that <m>v_2</m> and <m>v_3</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, but <m>v_1</m>
        is not an eigenvector of <m>A</m> because <m>Av_1</m> is not a scalar multiple of <m>v_1</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the <m>2</m>-eigenspace for the matrix
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 13 \amp 12 \\ 1/4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We need to find <m>\Nul(A-2I)</m>.  For this, we note
        <md><mrow>A - 2I = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 13 \amp 12 \\ 1/4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix}-2 \amp 13 \amp 12 \\ 1/4 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp -2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Now:
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
          \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-2 \amp 13 \amp 12 \amp 0 \\ 1/4 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1/2 \amp -2 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
          \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
          \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -32 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
        This gives us <m>x_1 - 32x_3 = 0</m> and <m>x_2 - 4 x_3 = 0</m> with <m>x_3</m> free.  The parametric
        vector form is
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}32x_3 \\ 4x_3 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix} = x_3 \begin{pmatrix}32 \\ 4 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, the <m>2</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is
        <md><mrow>\Nul(A-2I) = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}32 \\ 4 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>How many different eigenvalues can an <m>n \times n</m> matrix have?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The maximum is <m>n</m>.  Since eigenvectors for different eigenvalues are
          automatically linearly independent, and since no set of vectors in
          <m>{\bf R}^{n}</m> can be linearly independent if it has more than <m>n</m> vectors in
          it, we cannot have more than <m>n</m> different eigenvalues.  It is possible to
          have exactly <m>n</m> different eigenvalues, for example the <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix
          below has <m>3</m> different eigenvalues.
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the matrix <m>A</m> for reflection of <m>{\bf R}^2</m> about the line <m>y = 7x</m>.
      What are its eigenvalues?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The eigenvalues are <m>\lambda = 1</m> and <m>\lambda = -1</m>.  The matrix does not
        change any vector along the line <m>y = 7x</m>.  In other words, <m>Av = v</m> for every
        vector <m>v</m> along the line <m>y = 7x</m>. Therefore, <m>\lambda = 1</m> is an eigenvalue.</p>
      <p>The matrix flips every vector on the line through the origin that is
        perpendicular to the line <m>y = 7x</m>.  In other words, <m>Av = -v</m> for every
        vector <m>v</m> along the line <m>y = -x/7</m>.  Therefore, <m>\lambda = -1</m> is an
        eigenvalue.</p>
      <p>Since a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix can have at most <m>2</m> eigenvalues, we conclude that
        <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m> are the only eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      <p>Note that the eigenvalues and reasoning would be identical if we changed the
        line <m>y = 7x</m> to a different line <m>L</m> through the origin.  The eigenvalues
        would still be <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>, the <m>1</m>-eigenspace would be <m>L</m>, and the
        <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace would be the line through the origin perpendicular to <m>L</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Without doing any major computations, write one real eigenvalue for
      each of the following matrices.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}6 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ -4 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\ 6 \amp 6 \amp 6\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Since <m>A</m> is lower-triangular, its eigenvalues are its
            diagonal entries.  Each of these is <m>6</m>, so the only eigenvalue of
            <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda = 6</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The
            standard matrix <m>A</m> for the linear transformation <m>T</m> that satisfies
          <md><mrow>T(e_1) = e_3, \quad T(e_2) = 2e_2 - e_3, \quad T(e_3) = -e_2
              +2e_3</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We see <m>e_2+e_3</m> is an eigenvector corresponding to
              eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 1</m> since
          <md><mrow>T(e_2+e_3) = 2e_2-e_3 + (-e_2 + 2e_3) = e_2 + e_3</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}.</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The first two rows of the square matrix <m>A</m> are the
            same, so without doing any work we know that <m>A</m> is not invertible,
            therefore <m>\lambda = 0</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> since the equation
            <m>Ax = 0</m> has a non-trivial solution.</p>
        <p>Alternatively, we could have noted from the form of <m>A</m> that
            <m>A(e_1-e_2) = 0</m>, which shows that <m>\lambda = 0</m> is an eigenvalue
            and <m>e_1 - e_2</m> is a corresponding eigenvector.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>T: {\bf R}^2 \to {\bf R}^2</m> be the linear transformation
          which reflects across the line <m>L</m> drawn below, and let <m>A</m> be the
          standard matrix for <m>T</m>.  Answer both questions below without
          doing any computations.</p>
      <image source="images/q6_18s-line.pdf">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find all eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The equation of the line was not given, and it is
                irrelevant.  The eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m> and
                <m>\lambda_2 = -1</m>, since <m>A</m> fixes every vector along the line
                <m>L</m> while <m>A</m> flips every vector perpendicular to <m>L</m>.</p>
        <p>In the graph, <m>v_1</m> corresponds to <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m>, while
                <m>v_2</m> corresponds to <m>\lambda_2= -1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>For each eigenvalue of <m>A</m>, draw one eigenvector on
              the graph above.  Your eigenvector does not need to be perfect,
              but it should be reasonably accurate.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>We graph eigenvectors below.</p>
        <image source="images/q6_18s-answer">
          <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
          <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
        </image>
        <p>Many answers are possible: <m>v_1</m> can be any nonzero vector on
                <m>L</m> (going up-to-right or down-to-left), while <m>v_2</m> can be
                any nonzero vector perpendicular to <m>L</m> (going up-to-left or
                down-to-right).</p>
        <p>Note: Algebraically, the
                problem would have been much more painful!
        The line
                is actually the line <m>y = \dfrac{3}{7} x</m>, the matrix is
                <m>A= \dfrac{1}{29} \begin{pmatrix}20 \amp 21 \\ 21 \amp -20\end{pmatrix}</m>, and the eigenvectors
                drawn are <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}7/3 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 7/3\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix, and that <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are vectors
      in the <m>(-2)</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> and the <m>5</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, respectively.
      Find <m>A(8u - 4v)</m> in terms of <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We are given that <m>Au = -2u</m> and <m>Av = 5v</m>, so we use linearity:
        <md><mrow>A(8u-4v) = A(8u) - A(4v) = 8Au - 4Av = -16u -20v</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        There is one <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> with the property that
        <md><mrow>\Nul(A-2I) = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\},
          \quad \textup{ and } \quad
          \Nul(A+I) = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} \right\}</mrow></md>.
        Find <m>A</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We are told the <m>2</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is spanned by <m>\begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and that the
          <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is spanned by <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</m>, so
        <md><mrow>Au = 2u = \begin{pmatrix}14 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} \quad \textup{ and } \quad
          Av = -v = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Recall from section 3.3 that if <m>M</m> is any <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix, then its first
          column is <m>Me_1</m> and its second column is <m>Me_2</m> (this can also
          be seen directly from the definition of <q>matrix times vector</q> multiplication), so
          we need to find <m>A\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>A \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For this, we write the standard basis vectors as linear combinations of the
          eigenvectors <m>u = \begin{pmatrix}7 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</m>.
        <md><mrow>\textup{For } \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}: \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}7 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -3 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{RREF} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 3/22 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1/22\end{array}\right),
            \quad \textup{ so } \quad \dfrac{3}{22}u + \dfrac{1}{22} v
            = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>,
        <md>
          <mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}1</mrow>
          <mrow>0\end{pmatrix}
            \amp  = A \left(\dfrac{3}{22}u + \dfrac{1}{22} v\right)
              = \dfrac{3}{22} Au + \dfrac{1}{22} Av
              = \dfrac{3}{22} \begin{pmatrix}14</mrow>
          <mrow>2\end{pmatrix} + \dfrac{1}{22} \begin{pmatrix}-1</mrow>
          <mrow>3\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp   = \begin{pmatrix}41/22</mrow>
          <mrow>9/22\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md><mrow>\textup{For } \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}: \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}7 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp -3 \amp 1\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{RREF} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 1/22 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -7/22\end{array}\right),
            \quad \textup{ so } \quad \dfrac{1}{22}u - \dfrac{7}{22} v
            = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>,
        <md>
          <mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}0</mrow>
          <mrow>1\end{pmatrix}
            \amp  = A \left(\dfrac{1}{22}u - \dfrac{7}{22} v\right)
              = \dfrac{1}{22} Au - \dfrac{7}{22} Av
              = \dfrac{1}{22} \begin{pmatrix}14</mrow>
          <mrow>2\end{pmatrix} - \dfrac{7}{22} \begin{pmatrix}-1</mrow>
          <mrow>3\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp   = \begin{pmatrix}21/22</mrow>
          <mrow>-19/22\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Therefore,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}A\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp \\ \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \amp A\begin{pmatrix}0 \amp \\ \amp 1\end{pmatrix}\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}41/22 \amp 21/22 \\ 9/22 \amp -19/22\end{pmatrix}
          = \dfrac{1}{22} \begin{pmatrix}41 \amp 21 \\ 9 \amp -19\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>This problem's matrix <m>A</m> is an example of a diagonalizable matrix.  We will
      find a different way to compute <m>A</m> in section 5.4.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
</section>
